00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:18.609 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  everybody is an individual and everybody  is making decisions to benefit  themselves either as a consumer or as a  business person you know you won't you  want either more consumer goods and more  consumer satisfaction or you want higher  profits for your company so the  discipline of economics is very  individual oriented we talked a little  bit about external benefits and by the  way that is really the rationale for  subsidizing childcare that the benefits  to the children will benefit us all  eventually it's not just sticking with  those children and that's the rationale  for subsidizing education so from an  employer perspective childcare matters  because your employees have a place to  send their child and they're not worried  all during the day about what's  happening to that child and so peace of  mind for employees is very important to  productivity so it's a clear win-win all  of the research on Head Start shows you  the value of taking kids at a young age  and making them ready for kindergarten  and giving them a loving supportive  environment so we're going to save a lot  of money in law enforcement or going to  save money in remedial education it's  just so clear that it's an important  investment  [Music]  when I first came to the business school  this is 1972 Nixon had vetoed a child  care bill in 1971 which would have  provided a national system of child care  and his rationale was that it would  weaken the family so I knew this when I  came here but I had been doing research  on the economics of child care and my  very first faculty seminar at the  Business School was on the economics of  child care in which I argued or tried to  argue that subsidizing childcare would  be a worthwhile economic investment and  my colleagues all white men conservative  white men wouldn't let me give the  seminar they were so outraged at the  notion that I was proposing a subsidy  for childcare that they they just talked  among each other about how at rageous  this was and I was too new to tell them  to be quiet so I could speak and the  person who was running the seminar  didn't tell them to be quiet so I  realized early on that there was just  tremendous opposition to this idea it  would be a good investment for the  government subsidized childcare for  families who can't afford it and for the  rest of the population it would be good  to ensure good quality childcare a lot  of the childcare that exists now is not  good quality and so all the research is  done says that childcare is beneficial  to children but that research is done on  good quality childcare because poor  quality childcare doesn't let  researchers in oh we know that a lot of  the care is not good quality and we know  that this is not a good thing that our  children are being raised in in  situations that are not helping them to  grow and develop at a national level I  would like to see a childcare system we  don't have a childcare system we have  lots of people trying to get childcare  and one of the women who comes to talk  in my class is a GSB graduate she's  married to another GSB graduate their  issue is not financial they have enough  money but when she became pregnant she  put her child on a waiting list at a  childcare center the day she knew that  she was pregnant the child was born  approximately nine months later and she  wanted to go back to work three months  after that so it was almost a year that  the child had been on the waiting list  and they had no slot for the child it  didn't matter what she was willing to  pay there was no slot the other question  about childcare is affordability now  that was not her issue but many people  cannot afford the childcare that does  exist so we need a childcare system and  we needed to be subsidized for people  who who need the subsidy  [Music]  Fremont was proposing a ballot  initiative that would cost homeowners $1  a month $12 a year to provide  after-school childcare for Fremont  public school children and 86% of the  population voted this down and my  student was doing an exit poll because  we didn't know how the vote would come  but we were interested in why people  were voting however they were going to  vote had voted and what he found was  that most people said it's not my  problem if people don't have child care  they should figure it out I don't have  to pay $12 a year to help them figure  this out this is not this is not my  problem I think we need to look globally  we need to look beyond just our own  satisfaction or our country's  satisfaction because we are a global  economy we are a global entity and I'll  never forget when you know the  astronauts saw the Earth from the moon  and saw how we were a single entity and  pollution in one place affects pollution  and other and you know nuclear power  affects the whole world so I think all  of us need to be thinking more broadly  than ourselves or our families to our  country and then to the world as a whole  and I think that's the challenge of our  current moment is how to get us to think  more broadly  [Music]

